<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Teos</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Teos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian port city, home of the poet Anacreon, whose citizens fled Persian conquest to found Abdera and which later hosted the guild of Dionysiac artists.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Teos lay on an isthmus south-west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> and was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>, with Boeotian and Athenian elements according to tradition, c. 1000 BCE. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ionian-league">Ionian League</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> is said to have proposed Teos as the capital of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> because of its central position.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Teos lived from trade and two harbours. When the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> attacked c. 545 BCE, many citizens sailed away and founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abdera">Abdera</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anacreon">Anacreon</a>, the lyric poet, was born there. The 'Teian curses', public inscriptions of c. 470 BCE, threatened anyone who harmed the grain supply or plotted tyranny. Teos sent 17 ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Lade</a> in 494 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the Hellenistic kings Teos built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermogenes-architect">Hermogenes</a>'s great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and became the seat of the Ionian-Hellespontine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of Dionysiac artists. It passed into Roman Asia after 133 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/teos.jpg" alt="Teos" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Rebecca Georgia rebeccageorgia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%B0zmir,_Turkey_(Unsplash_fxXoSky-LU4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Teos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Dionysus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=teos" aria-label="Teos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5348 805 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Teos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5668 1061L6140 1251M5668 1061L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5668 1061h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5668 1061h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-566&amp;focus=teos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

